A||||4||Gary W||Fuel problem - need help||||5||
Z||000000||Gary W||10-21-2001||04:19 PM||||'86 GT - No fuel pressure on injector rail at cold start. Hooked up a test light to ALCL pin G - no light with key on. Apply 12V to pin G - pump runs. Crank the engine for awhile, light flickers and engine will run (oil pressure switch?). Idles OK until it warms up, stalls as soon as oil pressure drops below 40 on the gauge (test light on pin G goes out). If you keep the revs up, it will keep running. Tried new relay, checked the wiring all the way to ECM. With key on, I get 12V on the relay coil pins with a DVM, but it won't light a test light. Book says bad ECM - plus a bad oil pressure switch too? Anybody have anything else I can try?||||reg||5
Z||000001||maryjane||10-21-2001||05:45 PM||||fuel pump relay maybe. Gets it's sig from ecm<BR>tho as long as spark is there. Do you hear fuel pump kick in for 2 seconds when you first turn key to on? Search has a lot of threads on this, as does Ogre's cave.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-21-2001||05:51 PM||||It's entirely posible both are bad.<P>OP switch should stay on down to 4-5 PSI oil pressure I think.<P>The ECM operates a switch to keyed 12V. the current flow is ECM, Relay, Ground. The ECM may not normally provide enough current to light a test light but if you can feed 12V to the relay coil from another source and close the relay then I'd have to say the ECM is bad.<P>At least it's not the fuel pump...<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gary W||10-21-2001||06:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>It's entirely posible both are bad.<P>OP switch should stay on down to 4-5 PSI oil pressure I think.<P>The ECM operates a switch to keyed 12V. the current flow is ECM, Relay, Ground. The ECM may not normally provide enough current to light a test light but if you can feed 12V to the relay coil from another source and close the relay then I'd have to say the ECM is bad.<P>At least it's not the fuel pump...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If I hot-wire to the battery, the relay closes, so I guess I'm not getting enough juice from the ECM. I'm sure the OP switch is bad - it cuts out at low idle. Thanks for verifying my suspicions.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gary W||10-21-2001||08:30 PM||||I've been looking through the archives, and it looks like at least 3 others have had exactly the same problem. No power to the fuel pump relay. Anybody get it solved?||||reg||5
